1
00:00:04,639 --> 00:00:05,680
What's up nerds?

2
00:00:05,679 --> 00:00:08,240
I'm ***, the first instructor for the *** course.

3
00:00:08,240 --> 00:00:12,560
In order for us to get the best out of our agents, we need to understand how the agents themselves work.

4
00:00:12,560 --> 00:00:15,919
And that's why we're starting with lesson one, generating text.

5
00:00:15,919 --> 00:00:30,720
I know this might seem pretty basic, but hear me out because there's a lot of misconceptions that I would go as far as to say lies, that we just kind of make assumptions around and believe because it's easier to work that way, but I think understanding the details will make it easier to use these models more effectively.

6
00:00:30,719 --> 00:00:35,119
When you send a message to a model, it's important to understand what it gets and how it responds.

7
00:00:35,119 --> 00:00:36,479
It's not as simple as you think.

8
00:00:36,480 --> 00:00:41,520
You're used to sending a text to a person that they read, they think about, they look into some things, and then they respond.

9
00:00:41,520 --> 00:00:43,199
LLMs don't work that way.

10
00:00:43,200 --> 00:00:46,000
We make them act that way, but that's not how they work underneath.

11
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:54,960
The simplest way of explaining it is that the model makes informed guesses based on the information in the chat history, and it does this chunk by chunk, not response by response.

12
00:00:54,960 --> 00:01:01,519
It's not generating the perfect message based on the history, it's generating the perfect next token based on prior tokens.

13
00:01:01,520 --> 00:01:03,760
Let's start with a really simple first message.

14
00:01:03,759 --> 00:01:04,879
Subnerds.

15
00:01:04,879 --> 00:01:08,159
This message would be read by a person word by word.

16
00:01:08,159 --> 00:01:11,039
LLMs don't see words though, they see tokens.

17
00:01:11,040 --> 00:01:36,480
Tokens are the chunks of text that LLMs use to understand and make suggestions as to what comes next, because in the end, LLMs are effectively just autocomplete they generate new tokens based on the previous token for example let's take the sentence the capital of the US is blank if you hand this to a traditional completion API with an LLM it would then generate the next tokens, which in this case would be Washington DC.

18
00:01:36,480 --> 00:01:44,879
Notice how this is breaking things up though, with the dots, the spaces, and the letters sometimes being included, sometimes not.

19
00:01:44,879 --> 00:01:50,480
These are all details of how different tokenizers are implemented, and certain things work well for code, certain ones don't.

20
00:01:50,480 --> 00:01:54,799
A ton of iteration and innovation has happened in tokenizers over the last few years.

21
00:01:54,799 --> 00:02:06,879
None of that is super important to understand beyond the fact that models aren't seeing the specific paragraphs we send them, they're getting these chunks of text and then guessing what's most likely to be next based on what we've given them.

22
00:02:06,880 --> 00:02:09,680
Let's see how this looks in action with the simple demo with autocomplete.

23
00:02:09,680 --> 00:02:16,880
I'm intentionally using an old and kind of dumb model here, but thankfully can actually run on my computer to show the insides of how it works.

24
00:02:16,879 --> 00:02:23,039
Let's say we send a message like, write a JavaScript function that checks if a number is even, only code.

25
00:02:23,120 --> 00:02:25,439
The expectation is that we get back code.

26
00:02:25,439 --> 00:02:27,759
So let's see how the model actually does that.

27
00:02:27,759 --> 00:02:30,800
The first important thing to understand is what the model actually gets.

28
00:02:30,800 --> 00:02:39,840
It's not getting the text history, it's not getting a bunch of tokens, it's getting a specific text format that describes the state of the conversation and sets the model up to do the next thing.

29
00:02:39,840 --> 00:02:42,320
The format for the text here is called chat ML.

30
00:02:42,319 --> 00:02:46,800
There's a bunch of new formats since OpenAI since Open Sourced Harmony, which is a pretty good standard.

31
00:02:46,800 --> 00:02:49,120
The thing to notice here though is these call-outs.

32
00:02:49,120 --> 00:02:52,080
The IM underscore start and I am underscore end.

33
00:02:52,080 --> 00:02:58,159
These are special tokens that the models are aware of because they're used during training that allow different behaviors to happen at different times.

34
00:02:58,159 --> 00:03:03,840
Like for a user to send a message, the system to send a message, and then for the agent or assistant to respond to these messages.

35
00:03:03,840 --> 00:03:11,680
So while we send something that looks like traditional JSON wrapped in an array to the model over the API, it doesn't get these JSON blobs.

36
00:03:11,680 --> 00:03:14,800
It instead gets this custom format that it's using during training.

37
00:03:15,039 --> 00:03:16,159
The format is pretty simple.

38
00:03:16,159 --> 00:03:18,719
We have the indicator of a new message start.

39
00:03:18,719 --> 00:03:22,800
It immediately opens with the type of message, whether it's system, user, or assistant.

40
00:03:22,800 --> 00:03:26,800
We then give more context, in this case that it is Quen, it is a helpful assistant.

41
00:03:26,800 --> 00:03:30,240
We then have a new message after the end, which is a user message.

42
00:03:30,240 --> 00:03:32,800
This indicates that a user is sending this thing.

43
00:03:32,800 --> 00:03:35,840
And then the most important part, we hard code a start.

44
00:03:35,840 --> 00:03:38,800
We immediately say I am underscore start assistant.

45
00:03:38,800 --> 00:03:41,040
This is important because it's an autocomplete model.

46
00:03:41,039 --> 00:03:46,400
It has to generate the next token, so it wouldn't really know what to do if it just ended the user turn.

47
00:03:46,400 --> 00:03:54,400
So we hard code a start to the next turn so the model can do what it thinks makes the most sense next, which we can see when we press enter.

48
00:03:54,800 --> 00:03:58,960
Now that we have run this, we can see how the model responded with these percentages.

49
00:03:58,960 --> 00:04:00,560
The percentages might be confusing.

50
00:04:00,560 --> 00:04:06,240
The simplest way I can explain it is these percentages show how confident the model is with the given pick for the next term.

51
00:04:06,400 --> 00:04:15,920
Here it thinks that after our message asking it to write a JavaScript function that checks if a number is even, that the best next response is the word certainly with 66% confidence.

52
00:04:15,920 --> 00:04:18,880
Next it thinks sure it's a good option of 14%.

53
00:04:18,879 --> 00:04:22,960
Next, it thinks that three back ticks could be a good option with 14% as well.

54
00:04:22,959 --> 00:04:29,600
This is the option I would have preferred since we told it to only send code, but it wanted to be a helpful assistant, which steered it towards the word certainly.

55
00:04:29,600 --> 00:04:35,840
Next, it was relatively confident, like 92% confident, that an exclamation point was the best next thing to include.

56
00:04:35,840 --> 00:04:37,600
Then it suggested below.

57
00:04:37,600 --> 00:04:39,279
This little dot is for a space.

58
00:04:39,280 --> 00:04:41,199
So next I thought space below made sense.

59
00:04:41,199 --> 00:04:43,040
Then I thought space is made sense.

60
00:04:43,519 --> 00:04:49,280
yada yada and you can see all the other things it was considering before it responded with these particular words.

61
00:04:49,280 --> 00:04:52,240
It then continued generating and finished its response.

62
00:04:52,240 --> 00:05:01,280
And at the very end, if we had let it go all the way, it would hopefully have ended with one of these end tokens, thereby signifying to whatever tool is running this that the model can stop.

63
00:05:01,280 --> 00:05:03,199
Remember, the model has to stop eventually.

64
00:05:03,199 --> 00:05:04,800
It can't just autocomplete forever.

65
00:05:04,800 --> 00:05:08,080
So one of the things they're trained on is this idea of stops.

66
00:05:08,079 --> 00:05:11,600
They can respond with a certain phrase to trigger the end.

67
00:05:11,600 --> 00:05:13,120
That's not the only thing they can trigger.

68
00:05:13,120 --> 00:05:14,400
Remember this, we'll go back to it.

69
00:05:14,400 --> 00:05:23,360
One more small thing to showcase the non-deterministic nature of all this, but the weird patterns that happen underneath is asking Quantum 25 to generate a random number.

70
00:05:23,360 --> 00:05:28,879
It picks 8 with 20% confidence, 7 with 18%, 9 of 16%, etc.

71
00:05:29,040 --> 00:05:31,840
So its definition of random is not quite our definition.

72
00:05:31,840 --> 00:05:34,320
One more call out about the generation that it did here.

73
00:05:34,319 --> 00:05:38,720
You'll see that when it said console.log is even seven, it responded true.

74
00:05:38,720 --> 00:05:43,520
That is not a good response, because as I hope you understand, seven isn't even.

75
00:05:43,519 --> 00:05:51,600
The problem with this style of generation is that the model can't go back and correct itself because it's just outputting one token at a time appending to this history.

76
00:05:51,600 --> 00:05:53,920
It can't go back and say that's wrong and edit it.

77
00:05:53,920 --> 00:05:56,800
It has to just keep adding to the existing text corpus.

78
00:05:56,800 --> 00:06:01,120
This style of autocomplete responses allows for models to share information that they already know.

79
00:06:01,120 --> 00:06:08,160
So if you ask the model who was president in 1952, it's able to generate a response based on the information it was trained on.

80
00:06:08,160 --> 00:06:14,079
But then if you ask a modern smart model like Opus 5.5 what's today's date, you're not going to get a very good answer.

81
00:06:14,079 --> 00:06:16,880
It's actually going to answer hundreds of days behind.

82
00:06:16,879 --> 00:06:23,120
If you then ask it what the latest version of a package is, you're never going to get a correct answer, assuming it's been updated anytime recently.

83
00:06:23,120 --> 00:06:28,240
All of this is because this data has changed since the model was trained, or maybe it was just trained on old data.

84
00:06:28,240 --> 00:06:32,560
But when we use models and tools like Cloud Code, we don't see all of these types of issues.

85
00:06:32,560 --> 00:06:39,280
The reason is because the model can get that data other ways, not through its training, not through its intelligence, but through tools.

86
00:06:39,280 --> 00:06:43,920
In order to understand how agents get data, we have to do something that a lot of us haven't done in a while.

87
00:06:43,920 --> 00:06:44,879
Read some code.

88
00:06:44,879 --> 00:06:47,840
Jokes aside, I think it's important to understand all of this.

89
00:06:47,840 --> 00:06:52,399
I have some code here that allows a model response to be parsed and then run as bash.

90
00:06:52,400 --> 00:06:55,520
Remember what I said earlier about the model having to indicate when to stop?

91
00:06:55,519 --> 00:07:02,880
One of the reasons it wants to stop isn't just to finish responding, it's to generate a tool call that allows it to get data that it doesn't know.

92
00:07:02,879 --> 00:07:12,320
Like if it wants to know what the current date is, if it wants to know what files are in your code base, if it wants to know what packages are available on npm, the model has tools it can use to do that in most harnesses.

93
00:07:12,319 --> 00:07:18,640
And those tools have custom syntaxes the model can use in order to respond and trigger those types of behaviors and actions.

94
00:07:18,639 --> 00:07:22,560
In order for this to work, you need to stop generating once one of these tokens comes in.

95
00:07:22,560 --> 00:07:28,000
In this case, we have a simple example where stop sequence includes closing tag for bash.

96
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:33,840
What we're saying here is when we see this in the response, that means stop and we have to go do something.

97
00:07:33,840 --> 00:07:40,000
So the model will send something along the lines of bash ls close bash, which allows it to run this command.

98
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:41,520
And our code is pretty simple here.

99
00:07:41,519 --> 00:07:44,160
We grab the command out of that syntax.

100
00:07:44,160 --> 00:07:53,600
If we don't have a command, we just respond with the text, but if we do have a command, we output the command and then we run it with this sh helper to get the output of the command.

101
00:07:53,600 --> 00:07:56,800
And then importantly, we push this onto the messages.

102
00:07:56,800 --> 00:07:57,920
We'll talk about that in just a bit.

103
00:07:57,920 --> 00:08:01,200
So let's see this in action with a simple demo that I just threw together.

104
00:08:01,199 --> 00:08:08,240
The user sends what's in this directory and the model responds, oh, I can't see your file system, but it absolutely could.

105
00:08:08,240 --> 00:08:11,360
If it just responded with that format, it would work fine.

106
00:08:11,360 --> 00:08:12,640
So what's up?

107
00:08:12,639 --> 00:08:15,120
Why can't it see this data?

108
00:08:15,120 --> 00:08:17,040
The reason is actually quite simple.

109
00:08:17,040 --> 00:08:20,480
We didn't tell the model that it has access to this syntax.

110
00:08:20,480 --> 00:08:23,840
We can't just give it a tool and expect it to know how to use it.

111
00:08:23,840 --> 00:08:27,120
We have to tell it what the tool is and that it exists in the first place.

112
00:08:27,120 --> 00:08:31,200
Otherwise it has no idea that certain types of text can trigger certain behaviors.

113
00:08:31,199 --> 00:08:32,399
Here we have done just that.

114
00:08:32,399 --> 00:08:40,319
We added to the system prompt this tool prompt, which describes that the model is able to get data by using this format in order to run commands.

115
00:08:40,320 --> 00:08:50,319
So if we rerun this with this addition to the system prompt, you'll see the model will run these different commands in order to figure out what's in this project and then respond accordingly.

116
00:08:50,320 --> 00:08:54,000
This is a small JavaScript program called Cart, a shopping cart pricing module.

117
00:08:54,480 --> 00:08:55,680
yada, you get the idea.

118
00:08:55,680 --> 00:08:59,360
It's able to do all of this now because it knows that it has this tool it can call.

119
00:08:59,360 --> 00:09:00,960
And the bash tool is pretty powerful.

120
00:09:00,960 --> 00:09:03,439
It gives the model the ability to do a lot of different things.

121
00:09:03,440 --> 00:09:07,919
This also showcases the importance of the system prompt and how it steers model behavior.

122
00:09:07,919 --> 00:09:13,600
When the model has the ability to trigger certain behaviors or special syntaxes, it needs to know about it and know what it can do.

123
00:09:13,600 --> 00:09:18,000
And then once it does those things, it needs to get the results back so that it can continue operating.

124
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:20,480
This all starts with what we usually call a system prompt.

125
00:09:20,480 --> 00:09:23,199
In this case I'll call it system message because there can be more than one.

126
00:09:23,200 --> 00:09:28,079
In a simple example I tell the model that it can use bash to get info using the following syntax.

127
00:09:28,080 --> 00:09:32,639
We then follow up with a simple user message that says, what files are in this project?

128
00:09:32,639 --> 00:09:38,240
So when it is spun up to respond, it can take advantage of all of this data which steers it in a certain direction.

129
00:09:38,240 --> 00:09:42,480
One of the things the model can do is think, at least if it's a modern reasoning model.

130
00:09:42,480 --> 00:09:45,840
So it will think about what it has access to, what is being asked for.

131
00:09:45,840 --> 00:09:48,480
And remember earlier when I said the model can't correct itself?

132
00:09:48,480 --> 00:09:57,519
It can't if it says something wrong, but if it's talking to itself in the thinking traces and it gets something wrong, it can correct itself and just say, no, that's not right, I should go this other way.

133
00:09:57,519 --> 00:10:00,960
So by the time it gets to the output stage, it's much more likely to be correct.

134
00:10:00,960 --> 00:10:05,360
So after it thinks for a bit, it says, good question, let me check what files are here.

135
00:10:05,360 --> 00:10:07,279
And then it runs this bash command.

136
00:10:07,279 --> 00:10:09,039
But then I have this line drawn.

137
00:10:09,039 --> 00:10:11,680
I have this line drawn for a very important reason.

138
00:10:11,680 --> 00:10:13,200
We're done.

139
00:10:13,200 --> 00:10:20,799
After this response comes through and we hit our end token, which is this close bash, that is the end of the request.

140
00:10:20,639 --> 00:10:23,679
the API request that was made is over.

141
00:10:23,680 --> 00:10:27,519
The reason is because something has to happen before it can continue.

142
00:10:27,519 --> 00:10:32,079
We asked for this command to be run, so now we have to go run said command.

143
00:10:32,080 --> 00:10:43,199
Usually what this means is whatever's running this assistant, probably like a Cloud Code type tool, will fire off the command, get a response, and then send that response back as an append to the history.

144
00:10:43,200 --> 00:10:52,640
Every time an API call is made to the Cloud code servers, codecs, whatever else you're using, it usually will include the entire chat history because the model needs that in order to generate the next tokens.

145
00:10:52,639 --> 00:11:04,559
And when a tool call is made, the important thing to understand is that the response is run on whatever computer the harness is running on, and that response is appended to the history so the model can continue generating accordingly.

146
00:11:04,560 --> 00:11:11,040
The same way that you send a question to the model, the tool call sends a response to the model, which allows it to continue after.

147
00:11:11,039 --> 00:11:19,279
And the model then decides when to stop, whether it's stopping for a tool to be run or it's stopping because it's done doing whatever it has to do and it wants your response.

148
00:11:19,279 --> 00:11:23,759
The tool or the model chooses when to stop with one of these indicators and then you act accordingly.

149
00:11:23,759 --> 00:11:27,199
Let's talk a bit more about this part quick, the system message.

150
00:11:27,200 --> 00:11:32,079
The example I have here is relatively small, but this is not how things will work in the real world.

151
00:11:32,080 --> 00:11:34,079
I have another quick demo to show what I mean.

152
00:11:34,080 --> 00:11:42,639
I'm going to simply say hi to a handful of different tools, harnesses, whatever you want to call them, to see how they respond and specifically how many tokens are being used.

153
00:11:42,639 --> 00:11:49,120
If I send a simple say hi to Claude, it's not going to use the three tokens that say hi would be represented by.

154
00:11:49,120 --> 00:11:52,960
It's going to use a hell of a lot more, thousands even.

155
00:11:52,960 --> 00:12:03,040
I have tool search on, which means a lot of my tools, MCPs and stuff aren't included by default, just little bits of info that they exist, so the model knows they exist to go search for them with another tool.

156
00:12:03,120 --> 00:12:07,840
But it's still 21,000 tokens for what seems like a very simple request.

157
00:12:07,840 --> 00:12:11,360
Say hi, which it responded with, hi ***, what do you want to work on?

158
00:12:11,360 --> 00:12:12,879
Oh, how does it know it's me?

159
00:12:12,879 --> 00:12:19,360
It knows it's me because I have a Claude MD file that describes a handful of things that I wanted to know, including who I am.

160
00:12:19,360 --> 00:12:29,120
It also will do things like figure out what the current git status is and run a command before it sends the request to include that in the prompt so the model has all of this data before it even starts.

161
00:12:29,120 --> 00:12:32,480
Let's pull all this together with the next important thing to understand.

162
00:12:32,720 --> 00:12:33,439
Agents.

163
00:12:33,440 --> 00:12:34,639
What is an agent?

164
00:12:34,639 --> 00:12:43,919
The simplest way to put it is that an agent is some type of tool or system that allows for an LLM to run in a loop and make tool calls to get whatever information it needs, make changes, etc.

165
00:12:44,080 --> 00:12:45,919
The loop is the important part though.

166
00:12:45,920 --> 00:12:47,360
It doesn't just stop here.

167
00:12:47,360 --> 00:12:50,879
So with our example here, it just stops after the message runs.

168
00:12:50,879 --> 00:12:53,840
You'd have to go make another API request for this to continue.

169
00:12:53,840 --> 00:12:56,879
When I say that agents are loops, I mean it quite literally.

170
00:12:56,879 --> 00:13:02,960
Most of the harnesses we use to run agents are literally running in a for loop or a while loop of some form.

171
00:13:02,960 --> 00:13:07,040
In this case we have a simple for loop where I have a limit for turns at 40.

172
00:13:07,039 --> 00:13:08,960
I'll explain what that means in a sec.

173
00:13:08,960 --> 00:13:12,720
I also think this probably should have been the word step, not turn, but doesn't really matter here.

174
00:13:12,720 --> 00:13:18,319
This is the first message in the history, and as you can guess, we'll be appending new messages to this history as we go.

175
00:13:18,320 --> 00:13:24,000
So at the start of the loop, we call the Claud API with a request to create a message.

176
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:34,240
We say what model, how many tokens we want it to do at max, the system prompt, thinking, which is us turning on thinking saying that we want to get summaries of the results and that it's adaptive and then we give it tools.

177
00:13:34,240 --> 00:13:37,360
In this case we have a simple bash tool that allows it to run things.

178
00:13:37,360 --> 00:13:41,200
We then hand it messages, which is that same array from the top of the messages.

179
00:13:41,200 --> 00:13:43,680
It's arguably the only really important part here.

180
00:13:43,680 --> 00:13:45,280
Everything else could be kind of changed.

181
00:13:45,279 --> 00:13:46,319
This cannot be changed.

182
00:13:46,320 --> 00:13:48,879
The response is what we get back from Claude.

183
00:13:48,879 --> 00:13:52,080
So at the end here, we push that onto the history.

184
00:13:52,080 --> 00:13:58,799
In this case, we push it as role assistant because this is a response from the assistant, which is the model, and the content is whatever it's said.

185
00:13:58,879 --> 00:14:00,480
We have to decide what to do next.

186
00:14:00,480 --> 00:14:03,440
First we check to see if it is or in this case isn't tool use.

187
00:14:03,440 --> 00:14:08,320
Because if the model's stopping for a reason other than calling a tool, that is because it's done.

188
00:14:08,320 --> 00:14:11,919
Because we've only given it two reasons to stop, tool calls or being done.

189
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:16,799
So if it is the or so if it's not a tool call, that means it's done, so we early return and escape.

190
00:14:16,800 --> 00:14:19,920
That means from here on it has to be a tool call.

191
00:14:19,920 --> 00:14:21,679
So now we can assume accordingly.

192
00:14:21,680 --> 00:14:27,920
We grab the calls from response content filter where we filter based on if it is a tool call block or not.

193
00:14:27,920 --> 00:14:31,360
This is important because sometimes the model can respond with more than one tool call.

194
00:14:31,360 --> 00:14:41,840
Here we have results which is where we collect all of the results of the things we call because we're going to go through and safely parse and then get responses for all the different bash requests that it made.

195
00:14:41,840 --> 00:14:48,399
If any of these fail, we append to results with an invalid call saying it's an error and that the content is invalid input.

196
00:14:48,399 --> 00:14:54,000
Otherwise we rip the data, we run it, we get an output, and then we send this as a result.

197
00:14:54,000 --> 00:14:56,960
And at the end here, this is where things all come together.

198
00:14:56,960 --> 00:15:01,199
We push with role user the contents of those results.

199
00:15:01,200 --> 00:15:07,519
This means that we're taking all of the outputs from all of those tool calls and then appending them to the history like a normal message.

200
00:15:07,519 --> 00:15:15,679
So what the model would then see is the results of that tool call is a bunch of like bash outputs and it knows to continue working accordingly.

201
00:15:15,680 --> 00:15:19,040
And of course, we can wrap this up with one last demo.

