214
00:15:49,759 --> 00:15:51,039
It says what it did.

215
00:15:51,039 --> 00:15:59,919
And then when we run the tests after, they are correct, because the model was able to run a command here, this said command, to actually edit the files to fix the issues.

216
00:15:59,920 --> 00:16:12,240
So all this agent had was access to bash and it ran in a for loop so that every time it did something or requested a command to be run, we would run it, append that to the history, and then let the model generate the next thing.

217
00:16:12,240 --> 00:16:19,120
Whether that is another tool call, a explanation, some code that would be executed via tool call, all of those things, doesn't matter.

218
00:16:19,120 --> 00:16:25,039
The model is able to keep going until it tells us to stop or we hit our limit, which in this case was those 40 steps.

219
00:16:25,040 --> 00:16:27,679
Let's quickly go through the important takeaways from this lesson.

220
00:16:27,680 --> 00:16:31,439
First and foremost, the models aren't generating responses to our messages.

221
00:16:31,440 --> 00:16:34,879
They are autocompleting text based on the existing text history.

222
00:16:34,879 --> 00:16:38,720
If the thing doesn't exist in the history or the model's weights, it cannot use it.

223
00:16:38,720 --> 00:16:42,799
And this history is helpful in steering the model towards the right things in its weights.

224
00:16:42,800 --> 00:16:48,559
If the model is trained properly, it can use text outputs to request certain types of data through what we call tools.

225
00:16:48,560 --> 00:16:54,960
This allows them to go fetch things that they might not have, to run commands, to edit things, and to do stuff other than just output text.

226
00:16:54,960 --> 00:17:02,240
By doing these tool calls, it's able to put things in its own history and make changes in the world around it in order to make the next thing it does more accurate.

227
00:17:02,240 --> 00:17:05,600
All of this allows for the model to build its own context in history.

228
00:17:05,599 --> 00:17:09,600
And the vast majority of the context models work with are not what we told it.

229
00:17:09,599 --> 00:17:20,000
It's all of the data in the system prompt, all the tool calls and things that it's exposed to, and then it builds the context itself by using these tool calls to get the information it needs to make the next things it generates more accurate.

230
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:29,039
And by doing this in a loop, you end up with what we call an agent, a model that's able to use tool calls to build the context it needs and make real changes using all of the things we just discussed.

231
00:17:29,519 --> 00:17:30,400
That was a lot.

232
00:17:30,400 --> 00:17:40,000
And now that we understand it all, we can start taking advantage of it by learning more about the context and how we can manipulate it to make the models more successful in the things that we care about.